{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460241962,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-94a2b49d483861e1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"include":"emit_toylang.rs","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src","pattern":"Builtin::|Max|Sum|Tail|Flatten|fn emit|match which"},"output":"Found 5 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_toylang.rs:\n  Line 148: pub fn emit(file: &File) -> String {\n\n  Line 164: pub fn emit_module(module: &Module) -> String {\n\n  Line 490:         Expr::TailPipe { lhs, callee, .. } => {\n\n  Line 593:         Expr::TailPipe { lhs, callee, .. } => format!(\n\n  Line 642: /// Flattens a left-recursive `Pipe` chain (`a | b | c` parses as `(a | b) | c`, the same fold\n","metadata":{"matches":5,"truncated":false},"title":"Builtin::|Max|Sum|Tail|Flatten|fn emit|match which","time":{"start":1788460241945,"end":1788460241959}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The LLVM/C runtime represents Vec as a struct-of-arrays `tl_vec` with `len` and `ncols` and `cols[ncols][len]` of `int64_t`. A `Bool` is stored as... let me check. The `tl_vec_max` reads `v->cols[0][i]` which is `int64_t`. So a Bool is stored as 0/1 in an int64 slot (since `Bool => i64`... wait, `llvm_type` for Bool is `bool_type()` which is i1. But the Vec storage is int64 columns. Let me check how a Vec<Bool> is stored -- how `map`/`select` over a Vec<Bool> stores bools.\n\nActually, the key question: for `any`/`all` over a `Vec<Bool>`, how are the bools stored in the C runtime's `tl_vec`? They'd be int64 slots (0/1). So a C function `tl_vec_any(const tl_vec *v)` returns `int64_t` (0 or 1), iterating `v->cols[0][i]` and returning 1 if any nonzero, 0 if empty/all-zero. And `tl_vec_all` returns 0 if any zero, 1 if empty/all-nonzero.\n\nBut wait -- `Bool` in the LLVM type system is `i1` for a scalar, but inside a Vec it's stored as int64 (the column is int64). Let me verify by looking at how a Vec<Bool> is built or how `select`'s predicate result is stored. Let me check `compare` / how a Bool gets into a Vec. Actually `map(. >= 2)` produces a `Vec<Bool>`. The map body returns a Bool which is i1, but the Vec stores i64. Let me check the `map` emit in llvm to see if there's a zext. Let me look at how `tl_at`/columns handle bool. Actually, the simplest: `tl_vec_max` already works for the columns as int64, so bools stored as 0/1 int64 will work for `any`/`all` too.\n\nFor `first` over a Vec<T>: returns `tl_opt_some(v->cols[0][0])` if nonempty, else NULL. But for a record element, `first` should return the record pointer via `tl_rec_from_vec(v, 0)`. Like `tl_at(v, 0, 0, is_record)`. Actually `first` is exactly `tl_at(v, 0, 0, is_record)` -- the first element. Hmm, but that's for a 1-dimensional Vec. Since `first` operates on the outermost Vec, depth 0, index 0. Actually it's literally `tl_at(v, 0, 0, is_record)`. But that has the out-of-range check too (which for index 0 only triggers on empty). So `first(v)` == `v[0]!` essentially (index 0). But `first` returns `Opt<T>` and `v[0]` also returns `Opt<T>`. So actually `first(v)` is semantically identical to `v[0]` (index 0). \n\nWait, is that right? `v[0]` is `Index { base, index: 0, depth: 0 }` which yields `Opt<T>`. And `first(v)` yields `Opt<T>` = the first element or none. So yes, `first(v)` is exactly `v[0]` (index 0, depth 0). But `first` is a function call `first(v)` not `v[0]`. The semantic equivalence is a nice sanity check but they're different syntactic constructs.\n\nHmm, actually, should `first` just be a builtin that the checker could desugar to `Index{0}`? No -- the issue wants `first`/`any`/`all` as the cut operators of the search model, first-class. They're builtins. And `first` over a Stream (later) would be the early-exit, which is NOT the same as indexing. So keeping `first` as its own builtin is correct.\n\nOK so for the LLVM backend:\n- `first`: `tl_vec_first(const tl_vec *v, int is_record)` -> NULL if empty, else `tl_opt_some(is_record ? (int64_t)tl_rec_from_vec(v, 0) : v->cols[0][0])`. Or just reuse `tl_at(v, 0, 0, is_record)`. Actually, I could just emit `tl_at(arg, 0, 0, is_record)` reusing the existing runtime function. But `is_record` is determined by the element type. Let me just add a `tl_vec_first` for clarity, OR reuse `tl_at`. Reusing `tl_at` would be the simplest: `Builtin::First => self.call_rt(self.rt.at, &[arg, i0, i0_depth0, is_record_i32], \"first\")`. Hmm, but `tl_at` takes depth and is_record. For first, depth=0, index=0, is_record = whether elem is a record. That's exactly `tl_at(v, 0, 0, is_record)`. \n\nBut semantically `first` is \"cut\" not \"index\". Reusing `tl_at` conflates them. But it's the same operation on a Vec. I think for the C runtime it's fine to have a dedicated `tl_vec_first` OR reuse `tl_at`. Let me add a dedicated `tl_vec_first` for clarity and to keep the model clean, mirroring `tl_vec_max`.\n\nActually, the cleanest: `first` is the minimum-index reduction. Over a Vec, `first(v) = v[0]` semantically. Let me just add `tl_vec_first` that handles the record case. And `tl_vec_any`/`tl_vec_all` for the bool reductions.\n\nLet me check the is_record logic: for `first`, the element type is `T` (polymorphic). If `T` is a record, the first element is a record pointer gathered via `tl_rec_from_vec(v, 0)`. Otherwise it's `v->cols[0][0]`. So `tl_vec_first` needs `is_record` OR it can compute it from ncols... no, a one-field record also has ncols=1. So it needs `is_record` passed in, like `tl_at`. Actually wait, let me check: for a record with 1 field, ncols=1, and `tl_at` uses `is_record` to decide whether to gather. So `tl_vec_first` needs `is_record` too. Let me pass it.\n\nHmm, actually, simpler approach: since `first` is exactly index 0, I could just emit it as an Index node. But that changes the TIR shape (the node_types tag would be `selection.collapse` not `builtin.first`). The tags.rs would need to handle it. But the issue wants `first` as a first-class builtin with its own tag. So a dedicated `Builtin::First` is right.\n\nOK let me now look at the toylang self-emitter and the emit_toylang.rs builtin section, and the lua Helpers / go used tracking. Let me read the toylang emitter.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0688991f10016Qq9MjZ16BGCqo","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_068891897001mgbRKoFlDkfAY7"}}
