{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460454138,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-87f5fa47e76c47a7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_py.rs","newString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_max(v):\n    if len(v) == 0:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": max(v)}\n\"#;\n\n# The cut operator (gh:142): a reified search is a Vec, and `first` commits to the first answer.\n# An empty Vec has no first, so the absent Opt is tagged the way `tl_max` tags it.\nconst FIRST_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_first(v):\n    if len(v) == 0:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": v[0]}\n\"#;\n","oldString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_max(v):\n    if len(v) == 0:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": max(v)}\n\"#;\n"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -162,8 +162,16 @@\n         return \"none\"\n     return {\"some\": max(v)}\n \"#;\n \n+# The cut operator (gh:142): a reified search is a Vec, and `first` commits to the first answer.\n+# An empty Vec has no first, so the absent Opt is tagged the way `tl_max` tags it.\n+const FIRST_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_first(v):\n+    if len(v) == 0:\n+        return \"none\"\n+    return {\"some\": v[0]}\n+\"#;\n+\n /// Iterating characters rather than bytes, which agrees with the C runtime's byte loop because\n /// the two differ only above U+007F, where both pass the value through unchanged.\n const QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_quote(s):\n     out = ['\"']\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_py.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -162,8 +162,16 @@\n         return \"none\"\n     return {\"some\": max(v)}\n \"#;\n \n+# The cut operator (gh:142): a reified search is a Vec, and `first` commits to the first answer.\n+# An empty Vec has no first, so the absent Opt is tagged the way `tl_max` tags it.\n+const FIRST_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_first(v):\n+    if len(v) == 0:\n+        return \"none\"\n+    return {\"some\": v[0]}\n+\"#;\n+\n /// Iterating characters rather than bytes, which agrees with the C runtime's byte loop because\n /// the two differ only above U+007F, where both pass the value through unchanged.\n const QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_quote(s):\n     out = ['\"']\n","additions":8,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788460454136,"end":1788460454137}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now the Python emitter. Add `FIRST_HELPER` const (`tl_first`), and inline `any`/`all` with Python's `any()`/`all()` builtins. Add the dispatch entry. And the `expr` arms.\n\nPython's Opt representation: `\"none\"` / `{\"some\": v}`. So `tl_first`:\n```python\ndef tl_first(v):\n    if len(v) == 0:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": v[0]}\n```\n\n`any`: `any({expr})` -- Python's `any()` over an iterable of bools. Empty -> False. `all`: `all({expr})`. Empty -> True. \n\nWait, but Python's `any`/`all` check truthiness. `true`/`false` in Python are `True`/`False`. `any([True, False])` = True, `all([True, False])` = False, `any([])` = False, `all([])` = True. Good. So inline: `format!(\"any({})\", expr(...))` and `format!(\"all({})\", expr(...))`. But wait -- is there a name collision with the toylang `any`/`all` being emitted as `v_any`/`v_all` (user-prefixed)? No -- these are Python builtins called directly, and the toylang function names are prefixed with `v_` via `user()`. So `any(...)` refers to the Python builtin. Good.\n\nLet me add the Python FIRST_HELPER after MAX_HELPER and the dispatch.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0688ccb4e0014abo7kPt64uvsL","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_0688cb559001ien4O7KDYgHlqK"}}
