{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460472560,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-922b45d70c04a405","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_lua.rs","newString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_max(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n\";\n\n// The cut operators of the query-is-search model (gh:142). `first` commits to the first answer;\n// `any`/`all` reduce a `Vec<Bool>` to one Bool. Lua has no `any`/`all` over tables, so both are\n// spelled as loops that short-circuit the way the cuts do.\nconst FIRST_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_first(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  return { some = v[1] }\nend\n\";\n\nconst ANY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_any(v)\n  for i = 1, #v do if v[i] then return true end end\n  return false\nend\n\";\n\nconst ALL_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_all(v)\n  for i = 1, #v do if not v[i] then return false end end\n  return true\nend\n\";\n","oldString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_max(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n\";\n"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_lua.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_lua.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_lua.rs\n@@ -191,8 +191,32 @@\n   return { some = m }\n end\n \";\n \n+// The cut operators of the query-is-search model (gh:142). `first` commits to the first answer;\n+// `any`/`all` reduce a `Vec<Bool>` to one Bool. Lua has no `any`/`all` over tables, so both are\n+// spelled as loops that short-circuit the way the cuts do.\n+const FIRST_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_first(v)\n+  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n+  return { some = v[1] }\n+end\n+\";\n+\n+const ANY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_any(v)\n+  for i = 1, #v do if v[i] then return true end end\n+  return false\n+end\n+\";\n+\n+const ALL_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_all(v)\n+  for i = 1, #v do if not v[i] then return false end end\n+  return true\n+end\n+\";\n+\n const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_unwrap(v, depth)\n   if depth > 0 then\n     local out = {}\n     for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_unwrap(v[k], depth - 1) end\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_lua.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_lua.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_lua.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_lua.rs\n@@ -191,8 +191,32 @@\n   return { some = m }\n end\n \";\n \n+// The cut operators of the query-is-search model (gh:142). `first` commits to the first answer;\n+// `any`/`all` reduce a `Vec<Bool>` to one Bool. Lua has no `any`/`all` over tables, so both are\n+// spelled as loops that short-circuit the way the cuts do.\n+const FIRST_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_first(v)\n+  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n+  return { some = v[1] }\n+end\n+\";\n+\n+const ANY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_any(v)\n+  for i = 1, #v do if v[i] then return true end end\n+  return false\n+end\n+\";\n+\n+const ALL_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_all(v)\n+  for i = 1, #v do if not v[i] then return false end end\n+  return true\n+end\n+\";\n+\n const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_unwrap(v, depth)\n   if depth > 0 then\n     local out = {}\n     for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_unwrap(v[k], depth - 1) end\n","additions":24,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_lua.rs","time":{"start":1788460472555,"end":1788460472558}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait, there's a subtle issue with Python's `any`/`all`. The element of a `Vec<Bool>` -- how are booleans represented in Python? Let me check. In Python, `True`/`False`. `any([True, False])` = True. Good.\n\nBut wait -- is the `Vec<Bool>` a Python list `[True, False]`? Yes, the py emitter represents a Vec as a list. `any([True, False])` works. Good.\n\nNow the Lua emitter. Lua has no `any`/`all` builtins on tables, so I need helpers `tl_any`/`tl_all` AND `tl_first`. Let me add the consts, the `Helpers` fields, `builtin_helpers` entries, dispatch entries, and `expr` arms.\n\nLua Opt: `\"none\"` / `{ some = v }`.\n\n`tl_first`:\n```lua\nlocal function tl_first(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \"none\" end\n  return { some = v[1] }\nend\n```\n\n`tl_any`:\n```lua\nlocal function tl_any(v)\n  for i = 1, #v do if v[i] then return true end end\n  return false\nend\n```\n\n`tl_all`:\n```lua\nlocal function tl_all(v)\n  for i = 1, #v do if not v[i] then return false end end\n  return true\nend\n```\n\nWait -- in Lua, booleans are `true`/`false`. `if v[i]` checks truthiness. `true` is truthy, `false` is falsy. Good. Empty: `tl_any` returns false, `tl_all` returns true. \n\nBut there's a subtlety: Lua's only-false-and-nil-are-falsy. So `if v[i]` where `v[i]` is `true` -> truthy, `false` -> falsy. Good for booleans.\n\nNow let me add the Lua helpers. The Lua `Helpers` struct needs `first`, `any`, `all` fields. And `builtin_helpers` sets them. And the dispatch. Let me add the consts after MAX_HELPER.\n\nWait, in Lua, the boolean Vec elements -- are they `true`/`false` or `1`/`0`? Let me check how the lua emitter represents a Bool literal and comparison results. From the lua emitter, `Bool` is... let me check `bool_connectives` corpus or how `Logic`/`Compare` emit. Let me check the lua compare emit. Actually I recall the lua `Compare` emits `(a < b)` which yields `true`/`false` (Lua booleans). And `true`/`false` literals? Let me check how a `Vec<Bool>` literal `[true, false]` is emitted. The VecLit emits each item. A Bool literal... there's no Bool literal in toylang source actually (`true`/`false` aren't in the lexer Tok list -- I saw `Tok::And`, `Tok::Or`, etc. but no True/False). So a `Vec<Bool>` comes from `map(. >= 2)` which produces Lua booleans from comparisons. So the elements are `true`/`false`. Good, `tl_any`/`tl_all` work.\n\nLet me add the Lua helper consts.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0688d0e9a001xYzL6PGyV2N6bd","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_0688cece4001IjpJEy3J3S1q9k"}}
