Item=&bool>`. `|x| *x` dereferences. Or `|&x| x`. Let me use `|&x| x`. Actually `.iter().any(|x| *x)` is fine. Or `.iter().copied().any(|x| x)`. Hmm. Let me think about what's cleanest. Actually since the Vec is `Vec<bool>`, `v.iter().any(|x| *x)` works. But wait -- `self.expr(arg)` returns an expression; is it `&Vec` or owned? Looking at `Max => format!(\"tl_max(&{})\", self.expr(arg))` -- the `&` is prepended. So `self.expr(arg)` returns an owned expression and `&` makes it a reference. For `any`, I want `({expr}).iter().any(...)`. But the expr might be a literal `[true, false]` -- `[true, false].iter()...` works. Or a function call `v.foo().iter()`. Let me wrap: `format!(\"({}).iter().any(|x| *x)\", self.expr(arg))`. Hmm, but if expr is a borrow or a temporary... Let me look at how other inline reductions are done. Actually `Length => format!(\"(({}).len() as i32)\", self.expr(arg))` -- wraps in parens. So `format!(\"({}).iter().any(|x| *x)\", self.expr(arg))`. But the element is `&bool`, so `|x| *x` where `x: &bool`. `*x` is `bool`. `.any(FnMut(&bool) -> bool)` -- actually `.any()` takes `FnMut(&Self::Item) -> bool` where `Item = bool` for `Iter<bool>`, so the closure gets `&bool`. `|x| *x` dereferences. Good. Or `|&x| x` via pattern. Let me use `|x| *x`.\n\nWait, actually for `all` the same: `.iter().all(|x| *x)`.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: do I need `Clone` bound for `first`? `v.first().cloned()` requires `T: Clone`. The existing `tl_max` uses `T: Clone + Ord`. So `tl_first<T: Clone>` is fine.\n\nBut wait -- what about a `Vec<Record>`? `first` returns `Option<Record>`. `v.first().cloned()` -- `Record` needs `Clone`. Do the record types emitted by the Rust backend derive `Clone`? Let me check. The Rust emitter emits record types... let me check if they derive Clone. From the existing `tl_max<T: Clone + Ord>` and `tl_tail<T: Clone>` -- these require Clone. And `max` is restricted to Int/Int64 (which are `i32`/`i64`, Clone). But `first` is polymorphic over any element, including records. So `tl_first<T: Clone>` requires the record types to derive Clone. Let me check if the Rust emitter's record types derive Clone.\n\nLet me look at how the Rust emitter defines record types.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0688d85af001DfVed6fO46iXIk","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_0688d5a6a0012y741jVJ4baPhz"}}
