te `is_record` from the element type.\n\nLet me look at how the `Index` builtin arm computes `is_record`. Actually `Index` is a `Kind::Index`, not a `Builtin`. Let me look at how `tl_at` is called from the `Kind::Index` arm.\n\nActually, looking at the C `tl_at` signature: `int64_t *tl_at(const tl_vec *v, int64_t i, int64_t depth, int is_record)`. For `first`, I want `tl_at(v, 0, 0, is_record)`. But that reuses `tl_at`. Alternatively, a dedicated `tl_vec_first(v, is_record)`. Let me add a dedicated C function for clarity, mirroring `tl_vec_max`. But I could also just call `tl_at` with index 0, depth 0. Let me check how `Kind::Index` calls `tl_at` to mirror the `is_record` computation.\n\nLet me look at the `Kind::Index` emit in llvm.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0688ea0eb001wlnrbbWIpRB0kP","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_0688e8a6c001wYonIcWV2BXXT0"}}
