{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460622110,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-89cca04c596ae1f9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_llvm.rs","newString":"    vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_first: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_any: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_all: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n}","oldString":"    vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n}"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_llvm.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_llvm.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_llvm.rs\n@@ -79,8 +79,11 @@\n     vec_sort_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_reverse: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_first: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_any: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_all: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n }\n \n /// What a compiler-introduced binding holds.\n ///\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_llvm.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_llvm.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_llvm.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/emit_llvm.rs\n@@ -79,8 +79,11 @@\n     vec_sort_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_reverse: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_first: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_any: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_all: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n }\n \n /// What a compiler-introduced binding holds.\n ///\n","additions":3,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_llvm.rs","time":{"start":1788460622107,"end":1788460622109}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"`call_rt` returns a `BasicValueEnum` of whatever type the function returns. For `any`/`all`, if I declare the C function returning `i64`, the result is an i64 value. But the Tir type is `Bool` (i1). \n\nLet me check how the emitter handles the mismatch. Actually, looking at how `expr` is used: the `expr` function returns a `BasicValueEnum` and the type is tracked separately (via `t.ty`). When the value is consumed (e.g., by `show`, or stored into a vec, or used in a select), the consumer reads it according to the *type* not the actual LLVM type. \n\nFor `show(Bool)`: `build_select(value.into_int_value(), t, f, ...)`. `value.into_int_value()` -- if `value` is i64, this gives the i64. `build_select` requires the condition to be i1. If I pass i64, inkwell might error or LLVM might treat nonzero as true. Actually `build_select` in inkwell requires an IntValue that is i1 (it may not enforce, but LLVM IR `select` requires i1). Passing i64 to `select` would be invalid IR.\n\nHmm. So I need to truncate the i64 result to i1 for `any`/`all`. Let me check if there's a precedent. The `Compare` result is i1 (from `build_int_compare`). The `Logic` result is i1. So Bool values are i1 throughout. If `any`/`all` return i64, I'd need to truncate: `self.builder.build_int_truncate(i64_val, i1, \"bool\")`.\n\nAlternatively, make the C function return `int` (i32 in C) and declare it as i32 in LLVM... but then still truncate to i1. Or, simplest: declare `tl_vec_any`/`tl_vec_all` returning i64 and truncate in the emitter. Let me do that -- it's the cleanest.\n\nWait, but actually -- is there a simpler path? Let me reconsider. The `Bool` is i1 in llvm_type. But when stored in a Vec it's zext to i64. When read out (`read_slot`), it's truncated to i1. So a Bool value flowing in expressions is i1. So `any`/`all` should produce i1.\n\nSo in the `Builtin::Any` arm:\n```rust\nBuiltin::Any => {\n    let result = self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_any, &[arg], \"any\")?;\n    let i1 = self.ctx.bool_type();\n    self.builder.build_int_truncate(result.into_int_value(), i1, \"any_bool\").map_err(|e| e.to_string())?\n}\n```\nWait, but `build_int_truncate` returns `IntValue`, and I need `BasicValueEnum`. Let me check how `read_slot` does it -- it returns `.into()`. So `self.builder.build_int_truncate(...).map_err(...)?.into()`.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me check: does `call_rt` return the right type? For `vec_sum` (returns i64, type Int=i64), the result i64 flows directly and `show(Int)` does `call_rt(int_to_str, &[value])` which expects i64. Good.\n\nFor `any`, I'll declare `vec_any` returning i64, call it, then truncate to i1. Let me write:\n```rust\nBuiltin::Any => {\n    let v = self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_any, &[arg], \"any\")?;\n    self.builder.build_int_truncate(v.into_int_value(), self.ctx.bool_type(), \"any\").map_err(|e| e.to_string())?.into()\n}\n```\nWait, `build_int_truncate` returns `Result<IntValue, _>`. And `?` gives `IntValue`, then `.into()` to `BasicValueEnum`. But the whole arm needs to return `Result<BasicValueEnum, String>`. Let me check the return type of the `expr` match arm. The `Builtin` arm is inside `self.expr` which returns `Result<BasicValueEnum<'ctx>, String>`. Let me look at the signature.\n\nActually, the `Builtin::Max => self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_max, &[arg], \"max\")?` -- `call_rt` returns `Result<BasicValueEnum, String>`, and `?` unwraps to `BasicValueEnum`, and the arm evaluates to `BasicValueEnum`. So the arm's final expression is `BasicValueEnum`. For `Any`, I need the arm to evaluate to `BasicValueEnum`. So:\n```rust\nBuiltin::Any => {\n    let v = self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_any, &[arg], \"any\")?;\n    self.builder\n        .build_int_truncate(v.into_int_value(), self.ctx.bool_type(), \"any_bool\")\n        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?\n        .into()\n}\n```\nWait, `?` on `call_rt` gives `BasicValueEnum`, then `.into_int_value()` gives `IntValue`, then `build_int_truncate(...)?` gives `IntValue`, then `.into()` gives `BasicValueEnum`. The block evaluates to `BasicValueEnum`. But the arm needs `Result<BasicValueEnum, String>`? No -- the arm is `Builtin::Any => { ... }` where the block produces `BasicValueEnum` (not Result). But other arms use `?` which means the match is inside a function returning `Result`. Let me check: `Builtin::Max => self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_max, &[arg], \"max\")?` -- the `?` unwraps the Result, so the arm produces `BasicValueEnum`, and the whole match is in a `fn ... -> Result<BasicValueEnum, String>` context. So each arm produces `BasicValueEnum` (after `?`). So my `Any` block must produce `BasicValueEnum` and can use `?` internally. But `build_int_truncate` returns `Result<IntValue,_>`, so `?` gives `IntValue`, then `.into()` gives `BasicValueEnum`. But I also need the `call_rt` `?`. So:\n```rust\nBuiltin::Any => {\n    let v = self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_any, &[arg], \"any\")?;\n    self.builder.build_int_truncate(v.into_int_value(), self.ctx.bool_type(), \"any_bool\").map_err(|e| e.to_string())?.into()\n}\n```\nThis produces `BasicValueEnum`. Good.\n\nHmm wait, but `v.into_int_value()` -- `v` is `BasicValueEnum`, `into_int_value()` works if it's an int. `call_rt` returns `BasicValueEnum` which for an i64-returning function is `IntValue` wrapped. `into_int_value()` extracts it. Good.\n\nFor `All`, same pattern.\n\nFor `First`, it returns `Opt<T>` which is a pointer (`ptr.fn_type`). So `vec_first` returns `ptr` (a pointer to the opt box). Like `vec_max`. So:\n```rust\nBuiltin::First => {\n    let is_record = matches!(tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty_before_synth...), Some(Type::Record(_)));\n    ...\n}\n```\nWait, I need the element type. But `arg` has already been moved into `self.expr(arg)?` at the top of the `Builtin` arm? Let me re-read the builtin arm. Lines 1401-1455:\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n    let elem_ty = crate::tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).cloned();\n    let record_ty = arg.ty.clone();\n    let arg = self.expr(arg)?;  // arg moved here\n    match which {\n        ...\n        Builtin::Max => self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_max, &[arg], \"max\")?,\n        Builtin::Fields => self.fields_lit(&record_ty)?,\n    }\n}\n```\nSo `elem_ty` is captured before `arg` is moved. `elem_ty = runtime_elem(&arg.ty).cloned()` = the element type if Vec/Stream. For `first`, `elem_ty` is `Some(Type::Record(_))` for a record element. So `is_record = matches!(elem_ty, Some(Type::Record(_)))`. But wait, `first` only accepts Vec (not Stream), and `runtime_elem` returns the element for both Vec and Stream. But the checker refused Stream (since `first_call` uses `arg.ty.elem()` which is Vec-only). So `arg.ty` is `Vec<T>`, and `elem_ty = Some(T)`. Good.\n\nSo for `First`:\n```rust\nBuiltin::First => {\n    let is_record = matches!(elem_ty, Some(Type::Record(_)));\n    let is_record_val = self.ctx.i32_type().const_int(is_record as u64, false);\n    self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_first, &[arg, is_record_val.into()], \"first\")?\n}\n```\nThis returns the pointer (`BasicValueEnum`), same as `Max`. Good.\n\nFor `Any`/`All`, I need to truncate. Let me write them.\n\nNow let me add the `vec_first`, `vec_any`, `vec_all` to the `Runtime` struct, the `new` declarations, the C functions, and the emit arms.\n\nLet me first add to the Runtime struct.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0688f5e6b001QyJw7kUEmcLwe8","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_0688f1325001BONTdcrmuYM3iV"}}
