t element, so `first([3, 1, 4])` -> `3` (Opt<Int> present -> prints `3`). Wait, `Opt<Int>` present prints as the value `3`, absent prints `null`. Let me verify by looking at `max_empty.yaml` -> `max([])` -> `null`, and `max([3,1,4,1,5])` -> `5`. So `first([3,1,4])` -> `3` (present Opt prints as the value). Good.\n2. `first_empty.yaml` -- `first([])` needs a typed empty Vec. Like `max_empty.yaml`: `fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []` then `first(nothing())` -> `null`.\n3. `first_of_strings.yaml` or `first_records` -- `first` over a `Vec<Str>` or `Vec<Record>`.\n4. `any_basic.yaml` -- `any([true, false, true])` -> `true`. But wait, can I write `true`/`false` literals? The lexer has no `true`/`false` tokens (I saw `Tok::And`, `Tok::Or`, `Tok::Not`, but no True/False). So how do I write a `Vec<Bool>` literal? Let me check the `bool_result.yaml` or `bool_connectives.yaml` corpus to see how booleans are produced.\n\nLet me check how `Vec<Bool>` is produced in existing corpus -- probably via `map(. >= 2)` or comparison. Let me look at `bool_result.yaml` and `map_to_bool.yaml` (already saw: `[1,2,3] | map(. % 2 == 0)` -> `[false,true,false]`). So a `Vec<Bool>` comes from `map`. So `any([1,2,3] | map(. % 2 == 0))` -> `true`. Or `all(...)`.\n\nLet me design the corpus cases:\n1. `first_basic.yaml`: `first([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])` -> `3` (Opt present). Wait, the program `first([3, 1, 4])` -- `first` takes a `Vec<Int>`. Output `3` (Opt<Int> present prints as the value). Actually wait -- does `Opt<Int>` print as `3` or as `{\"some\": 3}` or `3`? Let me check `max` docs: `max([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])` -> output `5`. So `Opt<Int>` present prints as `5`. And absent prints `null`. So `first([3,1,4,1,5])` -> `3`.\n\n2. `first_empty.yaml`: `fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []` then `first(nothing())` -> `null`.\n\n3. `first_of_strings.yaml`: `first([\"red\", \"blue\"])` -> `\"red\"` -> output `\"red\"`. Wait, `Opt<Str>` present -- how does it print? `max` over strings is refused (max is Int/Int64 only). But `first` is polymorphic, so `Opt<Str>` present prints as the string. Let me check how `Opt<Str>` prints -- from `opt_unwrap` or the index. A present `Opt<Str>` prints as the string value (with quotes? or raw?). Let me check `unwrap_str.yaml` or similar. Actually, the printer for `Opt<Str>` present: the show function for Opt unwraps and shows the inner. For Str, `show(Str)` prints as a JSON string `\"red\"`. Let me verify by checking an existing corpus where an `Opt<Str>` is the result. `opt_is_absent_not_empty.yaml`? Let me just use a value that prints predictably. Actually, let me use `first([\"red\", \"blue\"])` and expect output `\"red\"`. But I'm not 100% sure of the quoting. Let me check `str_equality.yaml` or how a Str result prints. A top-level `Str` prints raw (no quotes) per the `emit_js`/`emit_py` \"top-level Str prints raw\". But an `Opt<Str>` is not a top-level Str -- it's an Opt. So it prints as JSON `{\"some\": \"red\"}`? No -- the Opt printer... let me check.\n\nHmm, this is getting uncertain. Let me check how `max` over Int prints: `max([3,1,4])` -> `3` (not `{\"some\": 3}`). So the Opt printer for a present Opt shows the inner value in JSON. For `Opt<Int>` present -> `3`. For `Opt<Str>` present -> `\"red\"` (JSON string with quotes). For absent -> `null`. Let me verify with a corpus case. Let me check `opt_unwrap_str.yaml` or `unwrap_str.yaml`.\n\nActually, let me look at how the test harness compares output. The `agreement_failures` compares the printed output string across backends. For `max([3,1,4])` the output is `3`. So the Opt<Int> present prints as `3`. For Opt<Str> present, it'd print as `\"red\"` (JSON quoted) since the show for Str produces a quoted string. Let me verify by checking an existing case with Opt<Str>.\n\nLet me search corpus for a case producing Opt<Str>. `opt_unwrap_str.yaml`? Let me check `unwrap_str.yaml`.\n\nActually, let me just keep it simple and avoid the Str quoting question by using Int for `first` cases, and for `any`/`all` use `Vec<Bool>` from `map`. Let me design:\n\n1. `first_basic.yaml`: `first([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])` -> output `3`.\n2. `first_empty.yaml`: `fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []` / `first(nothing())` -> `null`.\n3. `first_of_records.yaml`: `first([{n: 1}, {n: 2}])` -> output `{\"n\":1}`. Hmm, the record print format. Let me check `record_literal.yaml`. Actually this exercises the record-element path which is the interesting `is_record` case for the LLVM backend. Let me check how a record prints. From `record_literal.yaml`: `[{n: 1}]`... let me check. Actually let me look at `map_records.yaml` or `record_result.yaml`.\n\nLet me look at a couple corpus cases to nail the output format for records and Opt<record>.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068907ebd001Dq37otKeRc1mdz","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_068904337001LVLwH2HazoXo1c"}}
