{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460734145,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b6f02c90c2665eb0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"program: |\n  first([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])\n\noutput: |\n  3\n","filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/tests/corpus/first_basic.yaml"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/tests/corpus/first_basic.yaml","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"tests/corpus/first_basic.yaml","time":{"start":1788460734140,"end":1788460734143}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So `[\"ada\", \"bo\"][0]!` is `Opt<Str>` present, unwrapped to `Str`, and prints raw as `ada` (top-level Str prints raw). But `[\"ada\", \"bo\"][0]` (without `!`) is `Opt<Str>` and prints as... let me check. `[1,2,3][1]` is `Opt<Int>` present and prints as `2`. So `Opt<Int>` present prints as the value. `Opt<Str>` present -- would it print as `\"ada\"` (JSON quoted) or `ada` (raw)? The top-level-Str-prints-raw rule applies only when the program's result type is `Str`. For `Opt<Str>`, the result type is `Opt<Str>` (not `Str`), so it goes through the JSON printer, which would print `{\"some\": \"ada\"}`? No -- the Opt printer. Let me check how `Opt<T>` prints. Let me look at the `max_empty` -> `null` (absent). And a present Opt... `index_positive` `[1,2,3][1]` -> `2`. So `Opt<Int>` present prints as `2` (just the value, not `{\"some\": 2}`). Let me verify how the Opt printer works -- it probably prints `null` for absent and the inner value for present (no wrapper). Let me check the js show for Opt.\n\nLet me look at how the js show handles Opt (the `show` function for `Type::Enum` with as_opt). Let me check `emit_js.rs` show for Opt.\n\nActually, `max([3,1,4,1,5])` -> `5`, which is `Opt<Int>` present. So the Opt printer prints the inner value for present, `null` for absent. For `Opt<Str>` present, the inner Str is shown via `show(Str)` which produces a JSON-quoted string `\"ada\"`. So `first([\"ada\", \"bo\"])` -> `\"ada\"` (with quotes). Let me verify this is consistent across backends by checking how `show(Str)` works in the non-raw path. From `emit_js`, top-level Str prints raw via `console.log(body)`, but a Str inside an Opt goes through `show(Str)` which quotes it. Let me verify by checking `map_to_str` -> `[\"1!\",\"2!\",\"3!\"]` (quoted, since it's a Vec<Str>). So a Str inside a container is quoted. So `Opt<Str>` present -> `\"ada\"` quoted. Good.\n\nSo:\n1. `first_basic.yaml`: `first([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])` -> `3`.\n2. `first_empty.yaml`: `fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []` / `first(nothing())` -> `null`.\n3. `first_of_strings.yaml`: `first([\"ada\", \"bo\"])` -> `\"ada\"`.\n4. `first_of_records.yaml`: `first([{n: 1}, {n: 2}])` -> `{\"n\":1}`. (Record present in Opt prints as the record.)\n\nWait, but `first` over a `Vec<Record>` -- the record literal `[{n: 1}, {n: 2}]` is a `Vec<Record<{n: Int}>>`. `first` returns `Opt<Record<{n: Int}>>`. Present prints as `{\"n\":1}`. Let me verify the record print format: `record_literal.yaml` -> `{b: 2, a: 1}` outputs `{\"b\":2,\"a\":1}`. So `[{n: 1}, {n: 2}]`'s first element `{n: 1}` prints as `{\"n\":1}`. Good.\n\n5. `any_basic.yaml`: `any([3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2))` -> `true`. (Vec<Bool> = [true, false, true], any -> true.)\n6. `any_empty.yaml`: `any([] | map(. >= 2))` -- but `[]` can't be typed without annotation. Hmm. Let me use a function returning `Vec<Int>` then map. `fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []` / `any(nothing() | map(. >= 2))` -> `false` (empty Vec<Bool>, any -> false).\n7. `any_false.yaml`: `any([1, 1] | map(. >= 5))` -> `false` (all false).\n8. `all_basic.yaml`: `all([3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2))` -> `false` (not all true).\n9. `all_empty.yaml`: `all(nothing() | map(. >= 2))` -> `true` (vacuous).\n10. `all_true.yaml`: `all([2, 3] | map(. >= 2))` -> `true`.\n\nHmm, but I want to be careful about `map` over a `Vec<Int>` producing `Vec<Bool>` and then `any`/`all`. Let me verify `map(. >= 2)` produces `Vec<Bool>`. From `map_to_bool.yaml`: `[1,2,3] | map(. % 2 == 0)` -> `[false,true,false]`. Yes, `map` of a Bool body -> `Vec<Bool>`. Good.\n\nBut wait -- can `any`/`all` take the result of a pipe? `any([3,1,4] | map(. >= 2))` -- the `|` binds the subject, and `any(...)` is a call with argument `[3,1,4] | map(. >= 2)`. The arg is the pipe expression. `any_call` synthesises the arg. The pipe produces `Vec<Bool>`. Good. But actually, the call `any(arg)` -- the arg is `[3,1,4] | map(. >= 2)` which is an `Expr::Pipe`. `synth` handles `Expr::Pipe` -> produces `Vec<Bool>`. Then `any_call` checks `Vec<Bool>`. Good.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: `any` is called with the pipe as its argument. The parser: `any([3,1,4] | map(. >= 2))` -- `any(...)` with a parenthesized arg. The arg is `[3,1,4] | map(. >= 2)`. But inside parens, `|` binds. So the arg is the pipe. Good. But actually, `any` is a bare-callable (lowercase). `any([3,1,4] | map(. >= 2))` -- the `(` makes it a parenthesized arg. The `|` inside the parens is the pipe. Good.\n\nAlternatively, I could write `[3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2) | any(.)`? No -- `any` takes the Vec directly. Actually `any` could be used as `[3,1,4] | map(. >= 2) | any(.)` -- but `any` rebinds `.`? No, `any` doesn't rebind `.` (it's not `select`/`map`). So `any(.)` would need `.` bound. Hmm. Actually `any` takes a `Vec<Bool>` argument. `[3,1,4] | map(. >= 2) | any(.)` -- the last `|` binds `.` to the `Vec<Bool>`, and `any(.)` passes `.` (the Vec<Bool>) as the arg. That works too but is verbose. The parenthesized form `any([3,1,4] | map(. >= 2))` is cleaner. Let me use that.\n\nActually wait -- will `any([3,1,4] | map(. >= 2))` parse correctly? `any` is called with arg `([3,1,4] | map(. >= 2))`. The parens group the pipe. `synth` on the pipe -> `Vec<Bool>`. `any_call` checks `Vec<Bool>`. Good.\n\nBut hold on -- is `any` a bare callee? `bare_callee(name)` returns true for lowercase names. `any` is lowercase. So `any(...)` with parens is fine. And `any` followed by `(` -- the parser's `ident_expr` sees `next == Tok::LParen` -> argument_starts true, takes_argument -> the `(` starts on the same line -> true. So `any(arg)` is parsed as a call. Good.\n\nLet me also double check: the `any_call`/`all_call` use `synth(ctx, arg)` then check `Vec<Bool>`. For `any([3,1,4] | map(. >= 2))`, the arg is the pipe expr, `synth` produces `Vec<Bool>`, check passes. Good.\n\nNow let me also consider: should I test `first` over a `Vec<Record>` to exercise the `is_record` path in LLVM? Yes, that's the interesting case. Let me include `first_of_records.yaml`.\n\nLet me also consider the `refuses` cases for error messages:\n- `first(non_vec)`: `first(3)` -> \"first needs a Vec, found Int\". Let me add `first_needs_vec.yaml` with `refuses: true`.\n- `any(non_vec_bool)`: `any([1, 2])` -> \"any needs a Vec<Bool>, found Vec<Int>\". Let me add `any_needs_bool.yaml` with `refuses: true`.\n\nActually wait -- for `refuses: true` cases, every backend must refuse to run it (compile error). The corpus harness checks that every backend refuses. So these are compile-error programs. Good.\n\nLet me write the corpus cases. Let me keep it focused: a representative set.\n\n1. `first_basic.yaml` -- `first([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])` -> `3`.\n2. `first_empty.yaml` -- empty -> `null`.\n3. `first_of_strings.yaml` -- `first([\"ada\", \"bo\"])` -> `\"ada\"`.\n4. `first_of_records.yaml` -- `first([{n: 1}, {n: 2}])` -> `{\"n\":1}`.\n5. `any_basic.yaml` -- `any([3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2))` -> `true`.\n6. `any_false.yaml` -- `any([1, 1] | map(. >= 5))` -> `false`.\n7. `any_empty.yaml` -- empty -> `false`.\n8. `all_basic.yaml` -- `all([3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2))` -> `false`.\n9. `all_true.yaml` -- `all([2, 3] | map(. >= 2))` -> `true`.\n10. `all_empty.yaml` -- empty -> `true`.\n11. `first_needs_vec.yaml` -- `refuses: true`, `first(3)`.\n12. `any_needs_bool.yaml` -- `refuses: true`, `any([1, 2])`.\n\nHmm, that's 12 cases. Let me trim to the essential and most valuable. Actually, the AGENTS.md says \"Test behavior that matters, not the shape of the implementation.\" And \"every case runs on every backend.\" Let me keep a focused set:\n\n- `first_basic` (present Int)\n- `first_empty` (absent -> null)\n- `first_of_strings` (polymorphic, Str)\n- `first_of_records` (polymorphic, Record -- the is_record path)\n- `any_basic` (true)\n- `any_false` (false)\n- `any_empty` (false, vacuous)\n- `all_basic` (false)\n- `all_true` (true)\n- `all_empty` (true, vacuous -- the interesting one)\n- `first_needs_vec` (refuses)\n- `any_needs_bool` (refuses)\n\nActually let me cut `any_false` (covered by `all_basic` being false-ish? no). Let me keep the set but make each case earn its place:\n- `first_basic`: present, the happy path.\n- `first_empty`: absent -> null (the Opt-emptiness contract).\n- `first_of_records`: polymorphic + the is_record LLVM path.\n- `any_basic`: true.\n- `any_empty`: false (vacuous existential).\n- `all_basic`: false (one false among trues).\n- `all_empty`: true (vacuous universal -- the most interesting edge).\n- `all_true`: true (all trues).\n- `first_needs_vec`: refuses.\n- `any_needs_bool`: refuses.\n\nThat's 10. Let me also add `first_of_strings` for Str polymorphism? The record case covers polymorphism. Let me skip strings to keep it tight. Actually, let me keep `first_of_strings` too since Str is a different LLVM path (pointer, not is_record). Hmm, but `first_of_records` already covers the pointer-path (records are pointers). Str is also a pointer. So `first_of_records` covers the non-scalar path. Let me drop `first_of_strings`.\n\nWait, but for `any`/`all` I should also verify the `Vec<Bool>` comes out right. Let me make `any_basic` use `[3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2)` -> `[true, false, true]` -> `any` -> `true`. And `all_basic` use `[3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2)` -> `false` (not all true). And `all_true` use `[2, 3] | map(. >= 2)` -> `[true, true]` -> `true`.\n\nLet me write them. I'll write each yaml with the `program`, `output` (or `refuses`), and a placeholder `node_types` that `tag_corpus.rs` will regenerate. Actually, the `node_types` line is regenerated on every run by `tag_corpus.rs`. But the `support::cases()` uses `deny_unknown_fields` and `node_types` is read. If I omit `node_types`, `deny_unknown_fields` -- wait, `node_types` is a declared field with `#[serde(default)]`, so it's optional. But the AGENTS.md says \"a case never writes it [node_types], tests/tag_corpus.rs does, on every run.\" So I should NOT write `node_types` in my new cases -- `tag_corpus.rs` will add it. Let me check: `patch_node_types` either replaces the existing `node_types:` line or appends `\\n\\n{line}` if none exists. So if I don't write `node_types`, it gets appended. Good. So I'll omit `node_types` from my new cases.\n\nBut wait -- the `deny_unknown_fields` is on `Fields`, and `node_types` is a declared field, so it's fine either way. Let me omit it and let `tag_corpus.rs` add it.\n\nActually, re-reading AGENTS.md: \"`node_types` is different: a case never writes it, `tests/tag_corpus.rs` does, on every run.\" So I must NOT write it. Let me omit it.\n\nLet me write the cases. I'll create them with the `write` tool.\n\nFor `first_needs_vec`: `first(3)` -> the error is \"`first` needs a Vec, found Int\". But `refuses: true` cases don't pin the message (the corpus harness just checks every backend refuses). So I just need `refuses: true`. But the program must fail to compile (checker error), which makes every backend refuse. Good. But wait -- does the corpus harness's `agreement_failures` for `Refusal` check that `toylang::run_on` returns `Err`? Yes -- it runs `toylang::run_on(program, input, backend)` and if `Ok(out)` it's a failure. `run_on` calls `compile` first, which would return `Err` for a type error. So `run_on` returns `Err`, which is the refusal. Good.\n\nLet me write the cases.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0689114e8001lWGZAvVPYWVskS","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_0689095fe001ZW4sTmoWhNVDZb"}}
