{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460789430,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8da4b8ded5efe86a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/error.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/error.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use crate::ast::Span;\n2: \n3: /// Spans are recorded from the first commit because retrofitting them means touching every\n4: /// node that ever gets constructed. Nothing renders them against the source yet.\n5: #[derive(Debug)]\n6: pub struct Error {\n7:     pub span: Span,\n8:     pub msg: String,\n9: }\n10: \n11: impl Error {\n12:     pub fn new(span: Span, msg: impl Into<String>) -> Self {\n13:         Error {\n14:             span,\n15:             msg: msg.into(),\n16:         }\n17:     }\n18: }\n19: \n20: impl std::fmt::Display for Error {\n21:     fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n22:         write!(f, \"{} (at byte {})\", self.msg, self.span.start)\n23:     }\n24: }\n\n(End of file - total 24 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use crate::ast::Span;\n\n/// Spans are recorded from the first commit because retrofitting them means touching every\n/// node that ever gets constructed. Nothing renders them against the source yet.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Error {\n    pub span: Span,\n    pub msg: String,\n}\n\nimpl Error {\n    pub fn new(span: Span, msg: impl Into<String>) -> Self {\n        Error {\n            span,\n            msg: msg.into(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for Error {","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/src/error.rs","text":"use crate::ast::Span;\n\n/// Spans are recorded from the first commit because retrofitting them means touching every\n/// node that ever gets constructed. Nothing renders them against the source yet.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Error {\n    pub span: Span,\n    pub msg: String,\n}\n\nimpl Error {\n    pub fn new(span: Span, msg: impl Into<String>) -> Self {\n        Error {\n            span,\n            msg: msg.into(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for Error {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{} (at byte {})\", self.msg, self.span.start)\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":24,"totalLines":24,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/error.rs","time":{"start":1788460789426,"end":1788460789429}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me run `just check` (the fast loop). This will run the test suite excluding the docs mega-test. But first, let me run `just check` which is the prescribed fast loop. Let me run it.\n\nActually wait, I realize there's a potential issue with `all([2, 3] | map(. >= 2))` -- `[2, 3]` maps `. >= 2` -> `[true, true]` -> `all` -> `true`. Good.\n\nAnd `any_empty` -- `nothing() | map(. >= 2)` -> empty Vec<Bool> -> `any` -> false. Good.\n\nNow let me run `just check`. This will compile and run the tests. Let me run it with a long timeout.\n\nActually, before that, let me also create the docs reference pages so `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page` passes. The test `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page` checks that each name in `BUILTIN_NAMES` has a `docs/reference/builtins/{name}.md`. I added `first`, `any`, `all` to `BUILTIN_NAMES`, so I need `first.md`, `any.md`, `all.md`. But wait -- is `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page` part of `just check` (the fast loop) or only `just test`? Let me check. `just check` is \"the fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole.\" So it skips the docs mega-test (`every_fragment_is_a_real_program`), but `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page` is a separate test in `tests/docs.rs`. Let me check if `just check` runs `docs.rs` tests. The `just check` recipe skips the docs mega-test but may run other docs.rs tests. Let me check the justfile.\n\nActually, regardless, I need the docs pages for `just test` (landing) and the brief says docs pages are needed. And `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page` would fail without them. Let me create the docs pages now. They need to be real programs (the `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` harness checks code fragments in docs). Let me model them on `max.md`/`sum.md`.\n\nLet me write `first.md`, `any.md`, `all.md`.\n\nFor `first.md`:\n```markdown\n# first\n\n`first(v)`, of type `Vec<T> -> Opt<T>`: the first element of `v`, or `none` if `v` is empty. The\ncut operator of the query-is-search model (draft.md#query-is-search, gh:142): a reified search is\na `Vec`, and `first` commits to the first answer, abandoning the rest. Over a `Stream` the same\noperation is an early exit on the first element, which is the lazy/unbounded case this row does\nnot yet reach.\n\n```toylang\nfirst([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])\n```\n\n```output\n3\n```\n\nAn empty `Vec` yields the absent `Opt`, which prints as `null`:\n\n```toylang\nfn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []\n\nfirst(nothing())\n```\n\n```output\nnull\n```\n\nPolymorphic over the element type, so a `Vec<Str>` yields `Opt<Str>`:\n\n```toylang\nfirst([\"ada\", \"bo\"])\n```\n\n```output\n\"ada\"\n```\n```\n\nWait, but the docs harness `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` runs each fragment on every backend and checks they agree with the `output`. So the `first([\"ada\", \"bo\"])` -> `\"ada\"` must agree across all 7 backends. I've designed the emitters to produce that. Let me make sure the jq output for `Opt<Str>` present is `\"ada\"`. jq: `([\"ada\", \"bo\"] | if length == 0 then \"none\" else {some: .[0]} end)`. `.[0]` is `\"ada\"` (a string), `{some: \"ada\"}`. Then the jq printer for `Opt<Str>`... the show function. Let me check how jq shows an Opt. From `max` over Int: `max([3,1,4])` -> `3`. The jq show for `Opt<Int>` present -> shows `3`. For `Opt<Str>` present -> shows `\"ada\"` (quoted). Let me verify the jq show handles Opt. Actually, the jq emitter's `show` for Opt... let me trust it works like `max` (which is tested). The output `\"ada\"` should be consistent.\n\nBut wait -- there's a subtlety. The `output` for a docs fragment is compared across backends AND against the `output` fence. So if jq outputs `\"ada\"` (with quotes) and the fence says `\"ada\"`, that matches. But does the native (LLVM) backend output `\"ada\"` too? The LLVM show for `Opt<Str>` present -> shows the Str via `tl_quote` -> `\"ada\"`. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually let me reconsider the `first([\"ada\", \"bo\"])` docs fragment. The `output` fence is the *printed* output. For `Opt<Str>` present, the printed output is `\"ada\"` (the JSON-quoted string). But the top-level-Str-prints-raw rule only applies when the program's result type is `Str`. Here the result is `Opt<Str>`, so it goes through the JSON printer -> `\"ada\"`. Good.\n\nLet me write the docs pages. Let me keep them focused and real (every fragment runs).\n\nFor `any.md`:\n```markdown\n# any\n\n`any(v)`, of type `Vec<Bool> -> Bool`: true if any element of `v` is true; an empty `Vec` is false.\nThe existential cut of the query-is-search model (draft.md#query-is-search, gh:142): a `Vec<Bool>`\nis a reified search reduced to a mask, and `any` is whether the mask is set anywhere.\n\n```toylang\nany([3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2))\n```\n\n```output\ntrue\n```\n\nAn empty `Vec<Bool>` is false -- nothing is set:\n\n```toylang\nfn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []\n\nany(nothing() | map(. >= 2))\n```\n\n```output\nfalse\n```\n\nA `Vec<Int>` is refused, since `any` reduces a mask rather than a value of any other type:\n\n```toylang\nany([1, 2])\n```\n\n```error\n`any` needs a Vec<Bool>, found Vec<Int> (at byte 4)\n```\n```\n\nWait, the `error` fence pins the checker's message verbatim. Let me get the exact message. From `any_call`: `format!(\"`any` needs a Vec<Bool>, found {}\", arg.ty)`. The arg type is `Vec<Int>`. So the message is \"`any` needs a Vec<Bool>, found Vec<Int>\". But the error includes a span `(at byte N)`. Let me check the Error format. From the `max.md` error fence: `` `max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}> (at byte 4) ``. So the format is `<message> (at byte N)`. The span for `any([1, 2])` -- the arg is `[1, 2]` which starts at byte 4 (after `any(`). Let me check: `any([1, 2])` -- `a` at 0, `n` at 1, `y` at 2, `(` at 3, `[` at 4. So the arg `[1, 2]` starts at byte 4. The error span is `arg.span()` which is the arg's span starting at byte 4. So `(at byte 4)`. \n\nBut wait -- the `error` fence in docs uses `body.trim_end()` and compares `e.to_string()`. Let me check the Error `Display` impl to confirm the `(at byte N)` format. From `max.md`'s error fence: `` `max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}> (at byte 4) ``. So the format includes `(at byte N)`. Let me verify the exact Error Display. Let me check error.rs.\n\nActually, I already saw `max.md` uses `(at byte 4)`. Let me trust that format. For `any([1, 2])` the message is \"`any` needs a Vec<Bool>, found Vec<Int>\" and the span starts at byte 4. So `(at byte 4)`.\n\nHmm wait, but actually the arg's span -- `need_arg` returns the arg expr, and `any_call` uses `arg.span()`. For `any([1, 2])`, the arg is `[1, 2]` (a VecLit). Its span starts at `[` which is byte 4. So `(at byte 4)`. Good.\n\nBut actually, I need to double-check whether the docs `error` fence trims and matches exactly. From `docs.rs`: `Outcome::Error(body.trim_end().to_string())` and the check is `e.to_string() != *want`. So the error fence body (trimmed) must exactly equal `e.to_string()`. So I need the exact message. Let me verify by checking the Error Display and how `max.md`'s error matches. The `max.md` error fence is:\n```\n`max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}> (at byte 4)\n```\nAnd `max_call`'s message for `max([{n: 1}])` is \"`max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}>\" with span at byte 4. So `e.to_string()` = \"`max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}> (at byte 4)\". So the format is `<msg> (at byte <start>)`.\n\nFor `any([1, 2])`: msg = \"`any` needs a Vec<Bool>, found Vec<Int>\", start = 4. So `e.to_string()` = \"`any` needs a Vec<Bool>, found Vec<Int> (at byte 4)\".\n\nHmm, wait -- but is the span the arg's span or the call's span? `any_call` does `let arg_span = arg.span();` and `Error::new(arg_span, ...)`. So it's the arg's span. The arg `[1, 2]` starts at byte 4. So `(at byte 4)`.\n\nActually, let me double check by looking at the Error Display to confirm it's `(at byte N)` with the start offset. Let me check error.rs quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06891edd8001PBy5RhCCyN91nP","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_068919547001TdTlvzzDDclliH"}}
