{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460803670,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a94e52b8363d7d05","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# first\n\n`first(v)`, of type `Vec<T> -> Opt<T>`: the first element of `v`, or `none` if `v` is empty. The\ncut operator of the query-is-search model (draft.md#query-is-search, gh:142): a reified search is\na `Vec`, and `first` commits to the first answer, abandoning the rest. Over a `Stream` the same\noperation is an early exit on the first element, which is the lazy/unbounded case this row does\nnot yet reach.\n\n```toylang\nfirst([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])\n```\n\n```output\n3\n```\n\nAn empty `Vec` has no first, so the result is the absent `Opt`, which prints as `null`:\n\n```toylang\nfn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []\n\nfirst(nothing())\n```\n\n```output\nnull\n```\n\nPolymorphic over the element type, the way `max` is not: a `Vec<Str>` yields `Opt<Str>` rather\nthan being refused, and a `Vec` of records yields the first record whole.\n\n```toylang\nfirst([\"ada\", \"bo\"])\n```\n\n```output\n\"ada\"\n```\n\nA non-`Vec` is refused, since `first` cuts a reified search rather than reading a scalar:\n\n```toylang\nfirst(3)\n```\n\n```error\n`first` needs a Vec, found Int (at byte 6)\n```\n","filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/docs/reference/builtins/first.md"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/docs/reference/builtins/first.md","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"docs/reference/builtins/first.md","time":{"start":1788460803655,"end":1788460803667}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good, `(at byte {start})`. Now let me write the docs pages. Let me model on `max.md` / `sum.md`.\n\nFor `first.md`, I'll include: the happy path, the empty case, and the polymorphic Str case. For `any.md`: the true case, the empty case (false), and the refused `Vec<Int>` case. For `all.md`: the all-true case, the empty case (true, vacuous), and a false case.\n\nWait, but I need to be careful -- the docs `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` requires a `slow` fragment to exist somewhere (the assert `fragments.iter().any(|f| f.slow)`). That's already satisfied by existing Euler fragments. So I don't need to worry.\n\nAlso, the docs harness checks that EVERY backend agrees with the `output` fence. So my `first([\"ada\", \"bo\"])` -> `\"ada\"` must work on all 7 backends. Let me make sure the jq output for `Opt<Str>` present is `\"ada\"`. jq: `([\"ada\", \"bo\"] | if length == 0 then \"none\" else {some: .[0]} end)`. The result is `{some: \"ada\"}`. Then the jq show for `Opt<Str>` present... how does jq show an Opt? Let me check the jq show function for Opt. From the jq emitter, the show handles `Type::Enum` with as_opt. Let me check.\n\nActually, let me verify the jq show for Opt<Str> produces `\"ada\"`. Let me look at the jq emitter's `show`/`canonical` for Opt.\n\nHmm, I'm somewhat worried about edge cases in the docs fragments. Let me reduce risk: for the docs `first.md`, use Int examples (which I'm confident about from `max.md`). For `any.md`/`all.md`, use the `map(. >= 2)` examples. Let me avoid the Str docs fragment to reduce risk -- but I already have a `first_of_strings.yaml` corpus case that tests Str. The corpus harness runs all backends and checks agreement, so if there's a jq issue with `Opt<Str>` it'll surface there. So the corpus case is the safety net. For the docs, let me use Int for `first` to be safe, and keep the Str coverage in the corpus.\n\nActually, the corpus `first_of_strings.yaml` will be run by `just check` (the agreement harness) and if jq disagrees it'll fail. So let me just run `just check` and see. If the Str case fails, I'll fix the jq show for Opt<Str> or remove the case.\n\nLet me write the docs pages with Int examples for first, and the map examples for any/all.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the `first` docs page. Let me include:\n- `first([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])` -> `3`\n- empty -> `null`\n- refused: `first(3)` -> error\n\nFor `any.md`:\n- `any([3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2))` -> `true`\n- empty -> `false`\n- refused `any([1, 2])` -> error\n\nFor `all.md`:\n- `all([2, 3] | map(. >= 2))` -> `true`\n- `all([3, 1, 4] | map(. >= 2))` -> `false`\n- empty -> `true`\n\nLet me write them.\n\nWait, the error message for `first(3)` -- `first_call` message: \"`first` needs a Vec, found Int\". Span = arg.span() = `3`'s span. `first(3)` -- `3` is at byte 5? `f`=0,`i`=1,`r`=2,`s`=3,`t`=4,`(`=5,`3`=6. Wait: `first(3)` -- f(0) i(1) r(2) s(3) t(4) ((5) 3(6) )(7). So `3` is at byte 6. So `(at byte 6)`. Let me verify with `max.md`'s `max([{n: 1}])` -> `max` m(0) a(1) x(2) ((3) [(4)... so `[{n: 1}]` starts at byte 4. And `max.md` says `(at byte 4)`. Good. So `first(3)` -> `3` at byte 6. So error: \"`first` needs a Vec, found Int (at byte 6)\".\n\nHmm wait, let me recount `first(3)`: f=0, i=1, r=2, s=3, t=4, (=5, 3=6, )=7. Yes, `3` at byte 6.\n\nFor `any([1, 2])`: a=0, n=1, y=2, (=3, [=4. So `[1, 2]` starts at byte 4. Error: \"`any` needs a Vec<Bool>, found Vec<Int> (at byte 4)\".\n\nLet me write the docs pages.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068921c95001yg3W9L2hPxHHSk","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_06891ef7f001mX42etGequSp5Y"}}
