{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460841399,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a56075bac006e6a5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/tests/backend_llvm.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/tests/backend_llvm.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The native backend against the corpus.\n2: //!\n3: //! Native is not in `Backend::ALL` yet, because it cannot compile the whole language and a\n4: //! partial backend would turn the agreement harness permanently red. That absence would be a\n5: //! silent skip if nothing else watched it, so this file watches it: every corpus program is\n6: //! either compiled natively and checked against Lua, or listed by name in a snapshot of what\n7: //! native cannot do. The snapshot has to shrink at steps 5 and 6, and cannot quietly grow.\n8: \n9: mod support;\n10: \n11: use toylang::Backend;\n12: \n13: /// Everything native compiles must agree with Lua. Everything it does not is named here with\n14: /// the reason, so the gap is a tracked artifact rather than an absence.\n15: #[test]\n16: fn native_agrees_where_it_compiles() {\n17:     let mut supported = Vec::new();\n18:     let mut unsupported = Vec::new();\n19: \n20:     for case in support::cases() {\n21:         let (name, src, input) = (case.name, case.program, case.input);\n22:         let program = toylang::compile(&src).expect(\"corpus programs compile\");\n23:         match toylang::emit_llvm::to_ir(&program) {\n24:             Err(reason) => unsupported.push(format!(\"{name}: {reason}\")),\n25:             Ok(_) => {\n26:                 // Compared as results rather than as output, so a case that every backend has\n27:                 // to refuse is checked here too: both refusing is agreement, and one refusing\n28:                 // while the other runs is the disagreement worth catching.\n29:                 let native = toylang::run_on(&src, input.as_deref(), Backend::Native);\n30:                 let lua = toylang::run_on(&src, input.as_deref(), Backend::Lua);\n31:                 match (native, lua) {\n32:                     (Ok(n), Ok(l)) => assert_eq!(n, l, \"{name}: native and lua disagree\"),\n33:                     (Err(_), Err(_)) => {}\n34:                     (n, l) => panic!(\"{name}: native gave {n:?} and lua gave {l:?}\"),\n35:                 }\n36:                 supported.push(name);\n37:             }\n38:         }\n39:     }\n40: \n41:     assert!(\n42:         !supported.is_empty(),\n43:         \"native compiles nothing, so this test proves nothing\"\n44:     );\n45: \n46:     insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\n47:         \"compiles natively ({}):\\n{}\\n\\nnot yet ({}):\\n{}\",\n48:         supported.len(),\n49:         supported.join(\"\\n\"),\n50:         unsupported.len(),\n51:         unsupported.join(\"\\n\")\n52:     ));\n53: }\n54: \n55: #[test]\n56: fn emitted_llvm_ir() {\n57:     let program = toylang::compile(r#\"\"hello world\"\"#).unwrap();\n58:     insta::assert_snapshot!(toylang::emit_llvm::to_ir(&program).unwrap());\n59: }\n\n(End of file - total 59 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The native backend against the corpus.\n//!\n//! Native is not in `Backend::ALL` yet, because it cannot compile the whole language and a\n//! partial backend would turn the agreement harness permanently red. That absence would be a\n//! silent skip if nothing else watched it, so this file watches it: every corpus program is\n//! either compiled natively and checked against Lua, or listed by name in a snapshot of what\n//! native cannot do. The snapshot has to shrink at steps 5 and 6, and cannot quietly grow.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n/// Everything native compiles must agree with Lua. Everything it does not is named here with\n/// the reason, so the gap is a tracked artifact rather than an absence.\n#[test]\nfn native_agrees_where_it_compiles() {\n    let mut supported = Vec::new();\n    let mut unsupported = Vec::new();\n\n    for case in support::cases() {","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/tests/backend_llvm.rs","text":"//! The native backend against the corpus.\n//!\n//! Native is not in `Backend::ALL` yet, because it cannot compile the whole language and a\n//! partial backend would turn the agreement harness permanently red. That absence would be a\n//! silent skip if nothing else watched it, so this file watches it: every corpus program is\n//! either compiled natively and checked against Lua, or listed by name in a snapshot of what\n//! native cannot do. The snapshot has to shrink at steps 5 and 6, and cannot quietly grow.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n/// Everything native compiles must agree with Lua. Everything it does not is named here with\n/// the reason, so the gap is a tracked artifact rather than an absence.\n#[test]\nfn native_agrees_where_it_compiles() {\n    let mut supported = Vec::new();\n    let mut unsupported = Vec::new();\n\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let (name, src, input) = (case.name, case.program, case.input);\n        let program = toylang::compile(&src).expect(\"corpus programs compile\");\n        match toylang::emit_llvm::to_ir(&program) {\n            Err(reason) => unsupported.push(format!(\"{name}: {reason}\")),\n            Ok(_) => {\n                // Compared as results rather than as output, so a case that every backend has\n                // to refuse is checked here too: both refusing is agreement, and one refusing\n                // while the other runs is the disagreement worth catching.\n                let native = toylang::run_on(&src, input.as_deref(), Backend::Native);\n                let lua = toylang::run_on(&src, input.as_deref(), Backend::Lua);\n                match (native, lua) {\n                    (Ok(n), Ok(l)) => assert_eq!(n, l, \"{name}: native and lua disagree\"),\n                    (Err(_), Err(_)) => {}\n                    (n, l) => panic!(\"{name}: native gave {n:?} and lua gave {l:?}\"),\n                }\n                supported.push(name);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    assert!(\n        !supported.is_empty(),\n        \"native compiles nothing, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\n        \"compiles natively ({}):\\n{}\\n\\nnot yet ({}):\\n{}\",\n        supported.len(),\n        supported.join(\"\\n\"),\n        unsupported.len(),\n        unsupported.join(\"\\n\")\n    ));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn emitted_llvm_ir() {\n    let program = toylang::compile(r#\"\"hello world\"\"#).unwrap();\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(toylang::emit_llvm::to_ir(&program).unwrap());\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":59,"totalLines":59,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/backend_llvm.rs","time":{"start":1788460841376,"end":1788460841398}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Several issues:\n1. `backend_llvm` `emitted_llvm_ir` snapshot failed -- because I added the `declare` lines for `tl_vec_first/any/all` which changed the LLVM IR snapshot. But wait -- the snapshot is for a specific program. The `declare` lines are always emitted (declared in the module). So any program's LLVM IR now has 3 extra `declare` lines. The snapshot needs updating. But the brief says snapshots: \"for snapshot updates run the plain test so insta writes .snap.new files, then cargo insta accept\". The known denial is \"environment-variable-prefixed commands (VAR=x cmd never matches the allow-list -- run the plain command)\". So I run the plain test and then `cargo insta accept`. But wait -- is `cargo insta` allowed? It's not a VAR= prefix. Let me check. The denial list: \"environment-variable-prefixed commands (VAR=x cmd never matches the allow-list -- run the plain command; for snapshot updates run the plain test so insta writes .snap.new files, then cargo insta accept)\". So `cargo insta accept` is the prescribed way. Good.\n\nBut wait -- the `emitted_llvm_ir` snapshot is in `tests/backend_llvm.rs`. The snapshot pins the LLVM IR of a specific program. Adding 3 `declare` lines to every module changes that snapshot. I need to update it. But is this the right call? The `declare` lines are emitted unconditionally for every program (they're in `Runtime::new`). That means every LLVM IR snapshot would change. Let me check -- is there only one LLVM IR snapshot? Let me look at `backend_llvm.rs`.\n\nActually, the issue is that I declared `vec_first`, `vec_any`, `vec_all` as always-present `declare` lines in the module. But the existing `vec_max`, `vec_sum` etc. are ALSO always-declared, and they're already in the snapshot. So the snapshot already has `declare ... @tl_vec_max` etc. Adding 3 more declares changes it. So I just need to accept the snapshot update.\n\nBut wait -- is it correct that these are always declared? Yes, the existing pattern declares all runtime functions upfront in `Runtime::new`. So `vec_first/any/all` are always declared. The snapshot just needs the 3 new lines. Let me update the snapshot via `cargo insta accept`.\n\n2. `backend_llvm` and `backend_rust` `native_agrees_where_it_compiles` / `rust_agrees_where_it_compiles` failed with: \"corpus programs compile: Error { span: Span { start: 4, end: 10 }, msg: \"`any` needs a Vec<Bool>, found Vec<Int>\" }\". This is the `any_needs_bool.yaml` corpus case -- it's a `refuses: true` case. The `backend_llvm.rs` and `backend_rust.rs` tests apparently try to COMPILE every corpus program and fail on the refused ones. Wait, let me look at those tests. The error is \"corpus programs compile: ... `any` needs a Vec<Bool>\". So those tests iterate over corpus cases and expect them all to compile? But `any_needs_bool` is a `refuses: true` case that does NOT compile (by design). So those tests must be filtering out refused cases, OR the test expects all corpus programs to compile and the refused cases are... hmm.\n\nWait, the error message says \"corpus programs compile\" which suggests the test asserts compilation. But `any_needs_bool` is a refused case. Let me look at `backend_llvm.rs` and `backend_rust.rs` to understand. Maybe they only run cases that are NOT refused, or maybe the existing refused cases (`max_empty_unwrap_refuses`, etc.) are handled. Let me check how those tests handle `refuses`.\n\nActually, the existing corpus has `refuses: true` cases like `max_empty_unwrap_refuses`, `opt_unwrap_of_none_refuses`, etc. And `backend_llvm.rs` / `backend_rust.rs` presumably handle them. Let me look.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06892b8a0001lxi4yv1bBWZoay","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_068928d2d001hzQ0ByEhHmLjKR"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788460841620,"sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9adbcbfe54c9c972","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/tests/backend_rust.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/tests/backend_rust.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The Rust backend against the corpus.\n2: //!\n3: //! Rust joined `Backend::ALL` once it could compile the whole corpus, the same way native did.\n4: //! `tests/corpus.rs` now covers it like any other backend; this file is what watched the gap\n5: //! while it was still open, and stays as a snapshot of the \"not yet\" list -- empty for now, and\n6: //! it should only ever grow back temporarily, never silently.\n7: \n8: mod support;\n9: \n10: use toylang::Backend;\n11: \n12: #[test]\n13: fn rust_agrees_where_it_compiles() {\n14:     let mut supported = Vec::new();\n15:     let mut unsupported = Vec::new();\n16: \n17:     for case in support::cases() {\n18:         let (name, src, input) = (case.name, case.program, case.input);\n19:         let program = toylang::compile(&src).expect(\"corpus programs compile\");\n20:         let source = toylang::emit_rs::emit(&program);\n21: \n22:         let dir = tempfile::tempdir().expect(\"temp dir\");\n23:         let exe = dir.path().join(\"program\");\n24:         match toylang::link_rust(&source, &exe) {\n25:             Err(reason) => unsupported.push(format!(\"{name}: {reason}\")),\n26:             Ok(()) => {\n27:                 // Compared as results rather than as output, so a case that every backend has\n28:                 // to refuse is checked here too: both refusing is agreement, and one refusing\n29:                 // while the other runs is the disagreement worth catching.\n30:                 let rust = toylang::run_on(&src, input.as_deref(), Backend::Rust);\n31:                 let lua = toylang::run_on(&src, input.as_deref(), Backend::Lua);\n32:                 match (rust, lua) {\n33:                     (Ok(r), Ok(l)) => assert_eq!(r, l, \"{name}: rust and lua disagree\"),\n34:                     (Err(_), Err(_)) => {}\n35:                     (r, l) => panic!(\"{name}: rust gave {r:?} and lua gave {l:?}\"),\n36:                 }\n37:                 supported.push(name);\n38:             }\n39:         }\n40:     }\n41: \n42:     assert!(\n43:         !supported.is_empty(),\n44:         \"rust compiles nothing, so this test proves nothing\"\n45:     );\n46: \n47:     insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\n48:         \"compiles (rust) ({}):\\n{}\\n\\nnot yet ({}):\\n{}\",\n49:         supported.len(),\n50:         supported.join(\"\\n\"),\n51:         unsupported.len(),\n52:         unsupported.join(\"\\n\")\n53:     ));\n54: }\n\n(End of file - total 54 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The Rust backend against the corpus.\n//!\n//! Rust joined `Backend::ALL` once it could compile the whole corpus, the same way native did.\n//! `tests/corpus.rs` now covers it like any other backend; this file is what watched the gap\n//! while it was still open, and stays as a snapshot of the \"not yet\" list -- empty for now, and\n//! it should only ever grow back temporarily, never silently.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n#[test]\nfn rust_agrees_where_it_compiles() {\n    let mut supported = Vec::new();\n    let mut unsupported = Vec::new();\n\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let (name, src, input) = (case.name, case.program, case.input);\n        let program = toylang::compile(&src).expect(\"corpus programs compile\");\n        let source = toylang::emit_rs::emit(&program);","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-search-layer-build/tests/backend_rust.rs","text":"//! The Rust backend against the corpus.\n//!\n//! Rust joined `Backend::ALL` once it could compile the whole corpus, the same way native did.\n//! `tests/corpus.rs` now covers it like any other backend; this file is what watched the gap\n//! while it was still open, and stays as a snapshot of the \"not yet\" list -- empty for now, and\n//! it should only ever grow back temporarily, never silently.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n#[test]\nfn rust_agrees_where_it_compiles() {\n    let mut supported = Vec::new();\n    let mut unsupported = Vec::new();\n\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let (name, src, input) = (case.name, case.program, case.input);\n        let program = toylang::compile(&src).expect(\"corpus programs compile\");\n        let source = toylang::emit_rs::emit(&program);\n\n        let dir = tempfile::tempdir().expect(\"temp dir\");\n        let exe = dir.path().join(\"program\");\n        match toylang::link_rust(&source, &exe) {\n            Err(reason) => unsupported.push(format!(\"{name}: {reason}\")),\n            Ok(()) => {\n                // Compared as results rather than as output, so a case that every backend has\n                // to refuse is checked here too: both refusing is agreement, and one refusing\n                // while the other runs is the disagreement worth catching.\n                let rust = toylang::run_on(&src, input.as_deref(), Backend::Rust);\n                let lua = toylang::run_on(&src, input.as_deref(), Backend::Lua);\n                match (rust, lua) {\n                    (Ok(r), Ok(l)) => assert_eq!(r, l, \"{name}: rust and lua disagree\"),\n                    (Err(_), Err(_)) => {}\n                    (r, l) => panic!(\"{name}: rust gave {r:?} and lua gave {l:?}\"),\n                }\n                supported.push(name);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    assert!(\n        !supported.is_empty(),\n        \"rust compiles nothing, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\n        \"compiles (rust) ({}):\\n{}\\n\\nnot yet ({}):\\n{}\",\n        supported.len(),\n        supported.join(\"\\n\"),\n        unsupported.len(),\n        unsupported.join(\"\\n\")\n    ));\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":54,"totalLines":54,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/backend_rust.rs","time":{"start":1788460841615,"end":1788460841619}},"id":"prt_06892ba33001iOvVsf25CGWotG","sessionID":"ses_f977bc931ffe9OHrIOJvOWQPwa","messageID":"msg_068928d2d001hzQ0ByEhHmLjKR"}}
